{"qid":"Q221283","names":["Dutch Cape Coast"],"title":"Dutch Cape Coast","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Dutch Cape Coast (Kaap de Goede Hoop) was the colony established by the Dutch East India Company (VOC) at the southern tip of Africa in 1652 as a victualling and refreshment station for ships on the route to the Dutch East Indies. Under Jan van Riebeeck's initial administration and subsequent commanders, the settlement expanded beyond its intended limited scope as European settlers, the Boers, claimed land from Khoikhoi pastoralists. The VOC administered the colony until the company's bankruptcy and dissolution in 1799–1800, after which it passed under the Batavian Republic; Britain seized it in 1806 under the Treaty of Amiens reversal and confirmed possession at the Congress of Vienna in 1815, ending Dutch sovereignty definitively.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The colonial boundary of the VOC Cape Colony expanded continuously and was formally surveyed only episodically; early boundaries are inferred from land-grant records and are approximate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Cape Colony","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Cape-Colony"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Cape Colony","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cape_Colony"}],"britannica":"place/Cape-Coast","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Cape Coast","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Johannes van Valkenburgh","nativeName":"Johannes van Valkenburgh","roman":"","start":1656,"end":1659,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Jasper van Heussen","nativeName":"Jasper van Heussen","roman":"","start":1659,"end":1662,"